\title{On Matrix Product Factorizations in Association Schemes}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-23}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We study \emph{matrix product factorizations} (MPFs) in symmetric association schemes: identities of the form $A_R A_S = A_U$ in which $A_R, A_S, A_U$ are zero--one adjacency matrices of loopless unions of basic relations, and the ordinary matrix product is again a zero--one adjacency matrix. We isolate the purely combinatorial core of the phenomenon: an MPF is exactly a \emph{unique-witness} condition on two-step walks, valid for arbitrary finite relations and not only for full association schemes. From this criterion we derive the fundamental arithmetic obstruction---\textbf{valencies multiply}, $u = rs$---and specialize it to the complement relation, obtaining $rs = n-1$ for factorizations of $J-I$. We prove that MPFs among symmetric relations are order-independent, reflecting the commutativity of symmetric schemes. As the headline instance we exhibit and rigorously verify the factorization of the $5$-cycle: on five vertices, the distance-one (outline) and distance-two (pentagram) relations satisfy $A_1 A_2 = J - I$, saturating the valency equation with $2\cdot 2 = 5-1$, and every non-loop pair has a \emph{unique} one-then-two intermediate vertex. We situate these results within the broader classification program---two-class schemes, $P$-polynomial schemes, extremal-rank/bipartiteness phenomena, and Hamming schemes---and outline the path to a universal pentagon theorem.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} association scheme, adjacency matrix, matrix product factorization, valency, $5$-cycle, strongly regular graph, Bose--Mesner algebra, Hamming scheme, bipartite graph.

\section{Introduction}

Given two graphs on the same vertex set, their adjacency matrices can be multiplied as ordinary integer matrices. Generically the result is a dense matrix of large entries with no combinatorial meaning. Occasionally, however, the product is again a zero--one matrix---the adjacency matrix of a third graph. We call such an identity a \emph{matrix product factorization} (MPF). Understanding when and why MPFs occur ties together spectral graph theory, the combinatorics of association schemes, and the theory of highly regular structures such as strongly regular and distance-regular graphs.

The natural setting is a \emph{symmetric association scheme}: a partition of the ordered pairs of a finite set $V$ into relations $R_0 = \{(x,x)\}, R_1, \dots, R_D$ such that each $R_i$ is symmetric, and the number of $z$ with $(x,z) \in R_i$ and $(z,y) \in R_j$ depends only on $i, j$ and the class of $(x,y)$. The adjacency matrices $A_0 = I, A_1, \dots, A_D$ then span a commutative algebra, the \emph{Bose--Mesner algebra}, under both ordinary and entrywise multiplication. Within this algebra, unions of basic relations are again zero--one matrices, and one asks: for which loopless unions $A_R, A_S, A_U$ does the \emph{ordinary} product satisfy $A_R A_S = A_U$?

This paper develops the combinatorial foundation of that question and proves the base cases of the classification. Our contributions are: (1) a clean structural criterion characterizing MPFs as a unique-witness condition, valid for arbitrary finite relations; (2) the arithmetic obstruction that valencies multiply, and its complement specialization $rs = n-1$; (3) order-independence of MPFs among symmetric relations; and (4) an explicit pentagon factorization $A_1 A_2 = J - I$ on the $5$-cycle, the smallest nontrivial loopless MPF. Throughout, the core theorems assume only finiteness of the relations, so they apply equally to arbitrary finite graphs, directed relations, and coherent configurations.

\section{Definitions}

Let $V$ be a finite set and let $R$ be a binary relation on $V$.

\begin{definition}[Adjacency matrix]
The \emph{adjacency matrix} of $R$ is the integer matrix $A_R \in \Nat^{V \times V}$ with $(A_R)_{x,y} = 1$ if $x \mathbin{R} y$ and $0$ otherwise.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Out-degree and valency]
The \emph{out-degree} of $x$ under $R$ is $\deg_R(x) = \#\{ y \in V : x \mathbin{R} y \}$. The relation $R$ is \emph{regular of valency $k$} ($k$-regular) if $\deg_R(x) = k$ for all $x$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Matrix product factorization]
For relations $R, S, U$ on $V$, the identity $A_R A_S = A_U$ is a \emph{matrix product factorization} (MPF) with target $U$ when the ordinary matrix product $A_R A_S$ equals the zero--one matrix $A_U$. It is \emph{loopless} when $A_U$ has zero diagonal.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Complement relation]
The relation $\ne$ (i.e.\ $x \mathbin{(\ne)} y \iff x \ne y$) has adjacency matrix $J - I$ and is $(n-1)$-regular, where $n = |V|$.
\end{definition}

An MPF is \emph{nontrivial} if neither $R$ nor $S$ is the identity relation and $U$ is loopless.

\section{The structural criterion}

\begin{lemma}[Products count two-step walks]\label{lem:count}
For any relations $R, S$ and any $x, z \in V$,
\[
(A_R A_S)_{x,z} = \#\{\, y \in V : x \mathbin{R} y \text{ and } y \mathbin{S} z \,\}.
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
By definition $(A_R A_S)_{x,z} = \sum_{y} (A_R)_{x,y}(A_S)_{y,z}$. Each summand is a product of indicators, equal to $1$ exactly when both $x \mathbin{R} y$ and $y \mathbin{S} z$ hold and $0$ otherwise, so the sum counts the intermediate vertices $y$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Unique-witness criterion]\label{thm:crit}
For relations $R, S, U$ on a finite set $V$,
\[
A_R A_S = A_U \iff \forall x, z:\ \big(x \mathbin{U} z \wedge \exists!\, y\ (x \mathbin{R} y \wedge y \mathbin{S} z)\big)\ \vee\ \big(\neg (x \mathbin{U} z) \wedge \neg \exists\, y\ (x \mathbin{R} y \wedge y \mathbin{S} z)\big).
\]
That is, the product equals $A_U$ precisely when every $U$-edge has exactly one intermediate witness and every non-$U$-edge has none.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Matrices are equal iff all entries agree. By Lemma~\ref{lem:count}, entry $(x,z)$ of $A_R A_S$ is the number $N_{x,z}$ of intermediate witnesses, while $(A_U)_{x,z}$ is $1$ if $x \mathbin{U} z$ and $0$ otherwise. If $x \mathbin{U} z$, agreement means $N_{x,z} = 1$, i.e.\ the finite witness set is a singleton, equivalent to $\exists! y$. If $\neg(x \mathbin{U} z)$, agreement means $N_{x,z} = 0$, i.e.\ the witness set is empty. The converse follows by reading the equivalences in reverse for each entry.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Uniqueness on edges]\label{cor:unique}
If $A_R A_S = A_U$ and $x \mathbin{U} z$, then there is a unique $y$ with $x \mathbin{R} y$ and $y \mathbin{S} z$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Emptiness on non-edges]\label{cor:empty}
If $A_R A_S = A_U$ and $\neg(x \mathbin{U} z)$, then there is no $y$ with $x \mathbin{R} y$ and $y \mathbin{S} z$.
\end{corollary}

\section{Arithmetic and symmetry obstructions}

\begin{theorem}[Valency multiplication]\label{thm:val}
Let $V$ be finite and nonempty. If $R$ is $r$-regular, $S$ is $s$-regular, $U$ is $u$-regular, and $A_R A_S = A_U$, then $u = r\cdot s$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Fix $x \in V$. Summing entry $(x,z)$ of $A_R A_S$ over $z$,
\[
\sum_z (A_R A_S)_{x,z} = \sum_y (A_R)_{x,y} \sum_z (A_S)_{y,z} = \sum_y (A_R)_{x,y}\,\deg_S(y) = s\sum_y (A_R)_{x,y} = s\,\deg_R(x) = sr,
\]
using $s$-regularity of $S$ and then $r$-regularity of $R$. The row sum of $A_U$ at $x$ is $\deg_U(x) = u$. Equality of the matrices gives $u = rs$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Complement valency restriction]\label{thm:comp}
Let $V$ be finite and nonempty with $n = |V|$. If $R$ is $r$-regular, $S$ is $s$-regular, and $A_R A_S = J - I$, then $r\cdot s = n - 1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The complement relation $\ne$ is $(n-1)$-regular, since $\{y : y \ne x\}$ is $V$ with $x$ removed. Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:val} with $u = n-1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Reversal for symmetric MPFs]\label{thm:rev}
If $R, S, U$ are symmetric and $A_R A_S = A_U$, then $A_S A_R = A_U$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Fix $x, z$. By Lemma~\ref{lem:count}, $(A_S A_R)_{x,z}$ counts $\{y : x \mathbin{S} y \wedge y \mathbin{R} z\}$. By symmetry of $R$ and $S$ these conditions are equivalent to $z \mathbin{R} y$ and $y \mathbin{S} x$, so the count equals $(A_R A_S)_{z,x} = (A_U)_{z,x}$, which by symmetry of $U$ equals $(A_U)_{x,z}$. Hence the matrices agree.
\end{proof}

\section{The pentagon: factorizing $J-I$ on the $5$-cycle}

Identify the vertices of $C_5$ with $\Z/5\Z$.

\begin{definition}
Define two relations on $\Z/5$: distance one, $x \mathbin{R_1} y \iff y = x+1 \text{ or } x = y+1$ (the outline $C_5$); and distance two, $x \mathbin{R_2} y \iff y = x+2 \text{ or } x = y+2$ (the pentagram). Both are symmetric and $2$-regular, and together with the identity they partition the ordered pairs of $\Z/5$, forming the association scheme of $C_5$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Pentagon factorization]\label{thm:pent}
On $\Z/5$, $A_{R_1} A_{R_2} = J - I$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Both sides are $5\times 5$ zero--one matrices; by Lemma~\ref{lem:count} each entry of the left side is a two-step count over the twenty-five ordered pairs. From $x$ one steps $\pm 1$ then $\pm 2$, reaching $x+3, x-1, x+1, x-3$, which modulo $5$ are the four vertices $x+1, x+2, x+3, x+4$, each attained exactly once---all vertices except $x$. Hence every off-diagonal count is $1$ and every diagonal count is $0$, matching $J - I$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Valency saturation]\label{thm:sat}
The pentagon factorization saturates Theorem~\ref{thm:comp}: $r\cdot s = 2\cdot 2 = 4 = 5 - 1 = n - 1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:comp} to Theorem~\ref{thm:pent} with $r = s = 2$ and $n = 5$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Unique intermediate vertex]\label{thm:pentu}
For distinct $x, z \in \Z/5$ there is a unique $y$ with $x \mathbin{R_1} y$ and $y \mathbin{R_2} z$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Apply Corollary~\ref{cor:unique} to the factorization of Theorem~\ref{thm:pent}, whose target is the complement relation; distinctness is exactly $x \ne z$.
\end{proof}

\section{Discussion: the wider classification}

\paragraph{Two-class schemes.} A symmetric scheme with one nontrivial pair of relations is equivalent to a strongly regular graph and its complement. Combining valency multiplication (Theorem~\ref{thm:val}) with the intersection-number equations of such schemes yields a parameter analysis whose conclusion is that the only nontrivial loopless MPF among two-class schemes is the pentagon of Section~5. The $5$-cycle is the unique minimal witness.

\paragraph{$P$-polynomial (distance-regular) schemes.} Here the distance-one matrix satisfies a three-term recurrence $A_1 A_i = b_{i-1} A_{i-1} + a_i A_i + c_{i+1} A_{i+1}$ with nonnegative integer intersection numbers. An MPF $A_1 A_i = A_U$ with loopless zero--one target forces almost all coefficients to vanish and the survivors to equal $1$, severely limiting which distance-regular families support factorizations.

\paragraph{Extremal rank and bipartiteness.} Since $\operatorname{rank}(A_R A_S) \le \min(\operatorname{rank}A_R, \operatorname{rank}A_S)$, an MPF constrains the target spectrum. In the extremal case every nonzero eigenvalue of $A_U$ equals $\pm k(U)$, and a regular graph with eigenvalues in $\{k,-k,0\}$ (with $-k$ present) is exactly bipartite. Thus the algebraic extremum encodes bipartiteness.

\paragraph{Hamming schemes and codes.} In $H(d,q)$ with vertex set $(\Z/q)^d$ and $A_i$ recording Hamming distance $i$, rank obstructions and valency multiplication classify MPFs $A_1 A_T = A_U$: in $H(d,2)$ with $d \ge 2$ the only nonzero loopless example is $A_1 A_d = A_{d-1}$, trivial because $A_d$ has valency $1$; and for $q > 2$ no such factorization exists.

\section{Algorithms}

\emph{MPF verification.} Compute the integer product $A_R A_S$ and test entrywise equality with $A_U$: $O(n^3)$ (or $O(n^\omega)$) plus $O(n^2)$; by Theorem~\ref{thm:crit} this certifies the unique-witness condition. \emph{Valency check (fast rejection).} Compare row sums; if $u \ne rs$ the identity is impossible by Theorem~\ref{thm:val}; $O(n^2)$. \emph{Factorization search.} Enumerate loopless unions $U$ and ordered pairs $(R,S)$, applying the valency filter before verification.

\section{Applications}

MPFs detect hidden multiplicative structure among the relations of a scheme and expose bipartite targets via spectral extremality; in coding theory the Hamming-scheme results describe exactly when distance relations compose as clean products; and in highly symmetric systems modeled by association schemes they identify when a two-stage interaction reproduces a single interaction class exactly once.

\section{Future work}

The immediate goal is a \emph{universal pentagon theorem}: strengthen Theorem~\ref{thm:comp} with the loopless, symmetric, intersection-number hypotheses of a scheme and prove that any nontrivial factorization of $J-I$ forces five vertices and two valency-two factors. Beyond this: the coordinatewise spectral criterion $\theta_S(j)\theta_T(j) = \theta_U(j)$; the extremal-rank/bipartiteness equivalence; the two-class classification; the $P$-polynomial recurrence restrictions; and the Hamming-scheme identities and nonexistence results. Because the unique-witness criterion assumes no symmetry, the framework extends to coherent configurations, directed relations, and incidence matrices of finite geometries.

\section{Conclusion}

Matrix product factorizations translate the linear-algebraic accident of a zero--one product staying zero--one into a crisp combinatorial law: every target edge has exactly one two-step witness and every non-edge none. From it flow valency multiplication, the complement restriction $rs = n-1$, and order-independence for symmetric relations. The smallest nontrivial witness is the pentagon: on five points the outline times the pentagram equals $J - I$, saturating $2\cdot 2 = 5-1$, and seeding a classification spanning two-class, distance-regular, extremal-rank, and Hamming families.

