# Matrix Products That Stay Networks

Multiply two networks and you almost always get noise. But once in a while the product of two zero-one adjacency matrices is *again* a zero-one matrix &mdash; a third network hiding inside the arithmetic. These rare identities are called **matrix product factorizations** (MPFs), and their smallest nontrivial example is the humble pentagon.

## The one idea you need

When you multiply adjacency matrices, entry $(x,z)$ of the product counts **two-step journeys**: the number of intermediate vertices $y$ with an edge $x \to y$ of the first type and $y \to z$ of the second. So the product stays zero-one exactly when every target edge has *one* such witness and every non-edge has *none*.

<details><summary>Click to reveal the formal unique-witness criterion</summary>

$A_R A_S = A_U$ if and only if for all $x,z$: either $x \mathbin{U} z$ and there is a **unique** $y$ with $x \mathbin{R} y$ and $y \mathbin{S} z$; or $\neg(x \mathbin{U} z)$ and there is **no** such $y$. This is proved by comparing the two matrices entrywise, using that a finite witness set has size one iff it has a unique element and size zero iff it is empty.
</details>

## Meet the pentagon

Take five points on a circle. The **outline** joins each to its neighbours (distance one); the **pentagram** joins each to the two across from it (distance two). Their product is $J - I$ &mdash; *every* pair of distinct points. Try it yourself:

{{interactive_demo:0}}

Notice there is always exactly one intermediate vertex. That is the unique-witness criterion made visible.

## Why five, and only five

Search over cyclic networks and the pentagon stands alone. Multiply two single-distance circulant relations and compare with $J - I$:

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{visualization:2}}

Only $n = 5$ with distances $1$ and $2$ works. The reason is an arithmetic law every factorization must obey.

## The tollgate: valencies multiply

If the factors are regular with valencies $r$ and $s$, the target has valency $u = r \cdot s$. For a factorization of $J - I$ on $n$ vertices this forces $r \cdot s = n - 1$. The pentagon saturates it exactly: $2 \cdot 2 = 5 - 1$.

